export default "## Why Tracking Matters Even With Irregular Cycles\n\nThe instinct to stop tracking when cycles are unpredictable is understandable but counterproductive. Irregular data is still data. Over time, it reveals your personal range, your typical symptom patterns, and changes from your own baseline. That is more useful than comparing yourself to a textbook 28-day cycle you may never have had.\n\nPeople with PCOS often discover meaningful patterns after several months of tracking: which weeks tend to bring certain symptoms, how cycle length relates to stress or sleep, and which months seem to include ovulation based on BBT shifts.\n\n## What to Log and Why\n\n### Cycle dates\n\nThe non-negotiable. Record cycle day 1 every time. Even a cycle that lasted 67 days is a data point.\n\n### Symptoms\n\nPCOS symptoms are hormonal, which means they fluctuate throughout the cycle. Logging acne, energy, mood, and other symptoms daily, not just around your period, helps identify which hormonal phase drives which symptoms.\n\n### BBT\n\nOptional but valuable. A temperature chart shows whether ovulation occurred in a given cycle. With PCOS, many cycles are anovulatory; BBT tracking is one of the few ways to observe this at home.\n\n### Medications and supplements\n\nIf you are taking anything that affects hormones (birth control, metformin, inositol, thyroid medication), log it. Changes in medication often correspond to changes in cycle patterns.\n\n## Interpreting Irregular Data\n\nDo not try to apply standard cycle predictions to PCOS data. Instead, look for your personal patterns:\n\n- What is your typical cycle length range (e.g., 35 to 60 days)?\n- Do symptoms cluster in a particular phase?\n- Have any cycles included a BBT shift suggesting ovulation?\n- Are cycle lengths trending shorter or longer over time?\n\nThese questions are answerable with consistent tracking data. They are not answerable from a single cycle or from a predictive algorithm calibrated to regular cycles.\n\n## When to Talk to a Healthcare Provider\n\nThis guide is for educational purposes only and does not constitute medical advice. Bring your tracking data to a healthcare provider if cycles are absent for more than 90 days, if you have concerns about fertility, or if symptoms are affecting your quality of life. A log covering six or more months is the most useful artifact you can bring to that conversation.";
